    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d514">

                <pb facs="0523" n="515" sameAs="frus:frus1938v01/pg515"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">760F.62/323: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Bullitt</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-05-23T21:00:00+01:00">May 23, 1938—9
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received May 23—7:35 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>821. Massigli’s<anchor n="56" xml:id="d514-f1"/> description of the situation
                    this afternoon was that “The crisis continues—merely its first phase has been
                    successfully passed thanks to the military measures taken by Praha and to the
                    resumption of British-French solidarity at Berlin.”</p>
                <p>He said that the French Government has made no <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >démarche</hi> as yet today at Praha but that the French Minister there will
                    receive instructions this evening to urge: (1) that no further military measures
                    be taken by the Government; (2) that every effort be made to meet the demands of
                    the Sudetens; and (3) that negotiations with them be hastened with a view to
                    avoiding tension throughout the whole of the election period which does not end
                    until June 12.</p>
                <p>According to Massigli the British Minister at Praha has already been supplied
                    with new instructions to counsel conciliation and further concessions but he
                    does not know whether these instructions have been acted upon.</p>
                <p>On the whole he appeared sanguine and extremely pleased as he put it that the
                    first game of the set had gone to the French side. <pb facs="0524" n="516"
                        xml:id="pg516"/> He agrees that it is problematical that the British can
                    repeat their performance of Saturday<anchor n="57" xml:id="d514-f2"/> at Berlin
                    in the event of another acute rise in temperature.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bullitt</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
